Bill Gunshannon wrote:

>>And even in the computer biz, there are restrictions. Export
>>restrictions for national security, 
> 
> 
> Another good reason not to let the government get involved any more
> than they already are.  Do you know how many VAXen running Unix were
> found behind the Iron Curtain when it finally fell? (Both were
> restricted export items!)

And do you know how vital those VAXen were in actually enabling the Iron 
Curtain to fall?

In 1991 the hardliners' attempted a coup against Gorbachev failed 
because although they were able to control Moskva and Leningrad, they 
failed to monopolise communications and so secure the provinces and 
SSRs. They clamped down on the radio, television and phones, but left 
those funny beige objects surrounded by ineffectual looking geeks alone.

In the west the story of the KremVAX getting info of Jeltsin's decrees 
out via the university of Tampere (Finland) are well known, but far more 
vital was the way it enabled the regional governors to discover the 
illegitimacy of the coup and uncover the disinformation from the Kremlin.

The 1991 coup was patterned almost exactly on the 1964 coup against 
Khruschev, right down to the story of being taken ill at the holiday 
dacha. The difference? VAXen.

If ever there was an example of why it is in the US interest to do as 
they preach regarding free spread of ideas and technologies in support 
of democracy, this is it. Paranoia about dictators using X-boxen to 
develop missiles and other such reasons cited for restrictions on 
'National Security' grounds are utterly counterproductive- they surely 
do not work effectively against a dedicated foe and merely create 
unnecessary bad feeling amongst people with legitimate uses for the 
technology obstructed by such restrictions.


