Motif is now open sourced and royalty free except for commercial systems
that charge for the OS licence. So Motif is still "royalty" for VMS,
with the exception of Hobbyists who get VMS for free, and probably an
exemption for educational.

However, since Motif requires a licence on VMS, making the media freely
available even to the whole world wouldn't be a problem, especially when
you can officially get the actual source code of Motif (now at version
2.3) on the net.


Where there is a will, there is a way.

Instead of finding excuses NOT to provide downloadable media to
hobbyists, we should be working to find ways to get it done in an
acceptable way to HP.


Consider what Sun is doing to Solaris. VMS has to compete against Linux
too. If the owners of VMS think that Linux doesn't compete against VMS,
then they are doing the same mistake as those who thought Sun couldn't
steal VMS customers away, and later, those who though that Windows would
never be able to steal VMS customers (ironic that a few years later,
palmer did just that: move VMS customers to windows).


