In article <kIrwe.11209$U4.1430116@news.xtra.co.nz>, "Lurker" <nowhere@nothing.com> writes:
> "George Cook" <cook@wvnvms.wvnet.edu> wrote in message
> news:OnhtCM1f3JW0@wvnvms...
> 
>> In the case of the pyramids, what would happen to
>> the "absolute" property rights of the owners of all the tourist traps
>> around the pyramids if the pyramids were torn down and the land
>> converted into a trash dump?
> 
> Easy, they would still have their property. How much the value
> of the property was impacted is a different question but it would still
> be theirs.
> 

Their "absolute" property rights would have been impacted.  "Absolute"
property rights include the rights to the current value of the property.
Anything which impacts that value (without their approval) is a violation
of their "absolute" property rights.

My point, of course, being that the concept of "absolute" property
rights is absurd.

Putting aside "absolute" property rights, consider "normal" property
rights.  Does the pyramid owner have the right to install loud
sirens on the pyramids which make the environment in the tourist
trap area unbearable?  Do the tourist trap owners have the right
to start large fires which permanently blacken the pyramids with smoke?
Of course, many thousands of laws and court decisions cover all
these types of issues, but someone's property rights usually come
out ahead of someone else's (perfect outcomes/compromises are rare).

There will always be property right conflicts between owners of
property which is one of the main reasons we have governments and
"warped" courts.  To revisit Penn Station, the NY government/courts
had the right to decide (but failed to do so) that the property rights
of all the other property owners in NY were more important than the
property rights of one individual.  Of course governments/courts
should have some limits to their power, which is why the recent U.S.
Supreme Court decision is so wrong.  The decision basically says
that anything goes as far as governments ignoring individuals'
property rights.


George Cook
WVNET


