bill@cs.uofs.edu (Bill Gunshannon) writes:
> > The soviet union crashed for a lot of reasons, but certainly
> > not due to IP disregard. Plz.
> 
> There was no "property" in the Soviet Union.  There was no reward for
> excellence.  There was no reward for anything beyond the mediocre.  The
> result, after a few generations, was no innovation, no work beyond the
> bare minimum required and the eventual colapse as they could no longer
> compete with a world that was striving for excellence.  The result was
> inevitable.

There was property in the Soviet Union, people did certainly own their
cars (to stick with the examples used in this discussion) or house. In
theory all productive capital was owned by everyone. The problems of the
Soviet Union are rather because that did in practice mean something
quite different from what Marx' theory would have it: the whole country
was practically owned by an oligarchic group who used most of "public"
income to strengthen the army which they relied on to stay in
power. From the estimations I've read over 60% of the economic output
was used directly or indirectly for military personnel and
equipment in the late 80s. Now ask yourself if that could last.

Also there was a number of rewards for excellence, but since what
counted most was the advancment of military power, rewards were a little
biased towards stuff most people have no use for (I certainly don't care
about the recent improvements for tanks or military aircraft).

