bill@cs.uofs.edu (Bill Gunshannon) writes:
> How?  As I pointed out we shared software before the Gnu Public Virus
> just fine.  GNU has actually hurt the system as there are many people
> with great skills who will not touch GNU Software with a ten-foot pole
> who would provide their services if there was no such restriction (such
> as the BSD Style license).  Add to that the fact that it doesn't always
> accomplish what people think (I can point out software derived from
> GNU that in the form it is available, which meets the letter of the GPL,
> is totally useless form the standpoint of future development).

The GPL serves a number of purposes. As you cite the counter example of
the BSD licenses, I'd like to illustrate one of the shortcomings (in my
opinion) of the BSD style licenses and why the GPL suits my intentions
better:

1. The copyright notices of MS Windows quite clearly hint Microsoft used
a lot of code from the BSD TCP/IP stack.

2. I think we can agree that no operating system can survive in the
market today without a functional TCP/IP implementation.

3. There was a number of other TCP/IP implementations for Windows before
it was included in the base product. A few free (as in free beer) some
commercial (probably most also based on the BSD code, but that's not
important, the diversity of producers is).

Therefore the BSD license made it easy for Microsoft to eradicate their
competition in this particular field. The case might seem small and
unimportant but nevertheless I think it shows a pattern: If you are
content with a situation where others make huge profits from code you
produced and you don't see a cent (and possibly even have to pay to use
a product derived from your code) then go for it, publish under a BSD
style license. But I don't like this (although I'm not likely to write
something even remotely as successful as the BSD networking code), and I
don't want someone to profit from my work by charging others for it
although I originally published it free of charge.

As I said there is a number of aspects to the GPL, you are free to like
none of them. But don't make such general accusations of inadequacy
without providing any facts. People are free to stay 10feet and more
away from GPL code, I don't force them to contribute, but their implied
excellence of service is of no value to the GNU project if they don't
accept the GPL because it forms the legal basis of how GPLed code is to
grow independently from its original author.

