>> > do not offer this choice. 
>> 
>> And the have no responsbility to.  If you don't like it, go
>> elsewhere.  They don't hold a gun to your head and say "sign
>> here".
> 
> They are not forced to by the law. Please don't confuse justice and
> law or moral responsibility and legal duty. These are usually quite
> different from one another. Just let's say they are not forced to.

Eh?

I don't understand the point you're making here, are you suggesting they
should be forced to publically offer other rights? 


>> >                             And if this is the current law, 
>> 
>> It's called contract law.  Two parties are free to negotiate
>> whatever they want.
> 
> I don't know about the U.S. law. But here in Germany people are not
> allowed to freely negotiate anything in a contract. There's no way for
> anyone here to give up their fundamental rights like free speech,
> equal vote and a few more. Also contracts which grossly disadvantage
> one party are not legally binding here.

I'm no fan of click-through licensing schemes and other nonsense of such
ilk, but we're talking about VMS here.  People see a Windows license
certificate with its shiny hologram and promptly lose it.  Businesses
with VMS licenses file them away in a safe place, and if they're in
doubt about what they're entitled to they get a lawyer to look at them. 
Just like someone who has leased any other piece of property. 

You might like to compare software to the car leasing schemes that have
sprung up in recent years, and if you do a little research into it
you'll discover that it is backed by good folks like Volkswagen Bank. 
Do a few sums on how long people drive a car before getting a new one,
average mileage, low-end sell-on price, and the cost of financing. 
Whack a few percent on top and call it a lease.  Advertise like mad and
what's the German government going to do about it?  Nothing.  The car
might be good for ten years instead of the three you lease it for, the
value (i.e. amount to pay) they place on it at the end of the lease it
probably ten to twenty percent more than you'd get buying privately, and
if you tried to go to the bank to buy it in the first place, they
wouldn't give you a loan over the life of the car, heck not even half of
it. 

To many that's the same as software, it offers something for far less
than it actually costs to make.  The fact that you never own it doesn't
come into it.  As far as I'm concerned, the car analogy doesn't matter,
but I've had a good rant about it anyway. :-) 

<snip>

>> > *No* restrictions on the use of software, except pirating of
>> > course. 
>> 
>> That's strictly a contract matter.  I am sure if you were willing to
>> pay enough you could negotiate such a contract with most any company.
>> But to expect it for nothing really is kind of silly.
> 
> Fair treatment has always been a question of law-making. People
> somehow like to use any advantage they might have gained, unfairly or
> otherwise.

I call absolute bullshit on this and refer you to the original poster's
comment which sparked one of c.o.v.'s infamous monster threads and my
original response to it. 

This all started because someone who got a license to some software
***FOR FREE*** decided to offer it to all and sundry.  That was stupid,
and inconsiderate to all the other hobbyists who have been careful and
tried to keep to the spirit - if not the exact letter of the agreement
offered, for free, to allow access to VMS. 

Now, if I've been offensive to you I do apologise.  I've said some
stupid things in this thread, and I've let some of the disciples of (the
non-VMS) RMS get me a little heated about this with their
interpretations of what is the ideal licensing model.  I make my living
out of software that is licensed, and whilst I have nothing
ideologically against the GPL, I put a price on my time when I'm not
doing something for the sheer love of it. 


Doc.
