The concept of "absolute" individual property rights is absurd in the
extreme.  It entirely ignores the fact that more than one individual
exists (i.e., you can only have absolute property rights if no one
else exists).  In the case of the pyramids, what would happen to
the "absolute" property rights of the owners of all the tourist traps
around the pyramids if the pyramids were torn down and the land
converted into a trash dump?  Their property rights would be totally
destroyed in that their property would be made next to worthless and
their incomes, their hard work, success, "fruits of their labor", etc.
would all be taken alway. 

If the tourist trap owners had "absolute" individual property rights,
then they would have "TOTAL" control over the pyramids except for the
actual ownership (actually they could also control ownership (i.e.,
force a sale) if the pyramids were owned by someone whose reputation
(e.g. Saddam) might reduce their tourist value)).  The only remaining
right the pyramid owner would have would be the right to sell them
(but only to someone with a good reputation). 

>>> > In one sense it's just a zoning decision, but admittedly a very focused
>>> > one.
>>>
>>> And one that takes from one person for the gain of another.  Punishing
>>> one man for his accomplishments while rewarding another for nothing.

Any zoning decision takes and gives (sometimes in very unequal amounts).
Fifteen years ago my neighborhood was rezoned to keep slum lords from
converting single family homes to apartments.  The slum lords lost big
while most of the remaining home owners eventualy came out way ahead
although some (the ones who wanted to sale to the slum lords) lost in
the short term.  I supported the rezoning because it protected my
property rights by protecting the value of my property.  My home is
now worth well over twice what it was and two of the four houses
bordering mine are single family.  If not for the rezoning, I would
be complelely surrounded by multi-student (we are a university town) 
apartments and my house would have a fraction of its current value,
and the area would most likely have lost its national historical
district status.

In our town's warehouse district (another national historical district),
someone recently purchased one of the few remaining cold storage
warehouses of its type in the country.  He wants to tear it down,
but if he does, the area will lose its national historical district
status.  If the national historical district is lost, then owners
of other buildings in the area who have used large tax credits
(multi-year credits reaching into the future) to restore their
buildings will lose those credits.  Whose property rights should
prevail?  Who would you punish?  Who would you reward?  Should the
owners of the restored buildings have the "fruits of their labor"
taken away by a newcomer out to make a fast buck?

Recently in our downtown national historical district, the University
was going to tear down an old masonic lodge to create a few parking
spaces.  Doing so would have damaged the character of the entire
historical district (in that the building sits at the head of the main
street).  Fortunately wiser heads prevailed, or the properly values
(aka property rights) of many other properties would have been affected.

As far as GCT and Penn Station, it is generally now considered to have
been a mistake tearing Penn Station down.  Many still mourn it.  Not
long ago an archaeological survey of its remains took place at its
dumping grounds.  It's unfortunate when our nation's history must be
studied from that perspective.  One of the main problems faced by
preservationists is that governments and private owners often only
look at the value of their property in the extremely short term.  It
was only a few years after Penn Station came down, that NY realized
far too late the mistake it had made.

FWIW, I also think the liberals (they were the majority) on the U.S.
Supreme Court are wrong in the New London, CT case.  Telling someone
they can't tear a building down is one thing, but tearing it down
against their will in order to give the land to someone else for
personal profit is quite another.


George Cook
WVNET


