VMS is not an operating system that was designed for the stupid.
It was designed to assist people who needed computing work done
as part of their jobs.  There was never a goal of making
an installation easy for everybody who would log into the
system.  VMS is a timesharing OS (or tries to be) and is
designed to deliver computing srevices to hundreds and thousands
of people who are logged in _at the same time_; none of these
users are supposed to be able to tell from the system's response
that there is anybody else using that system.

It is not necessary for each of these thousands of users to
know how to cold start the system.  I would even declare this
a non-goal but I may have to change my attitude if the only
way to keep this knowledge is through hobbyists.  I would
still tradeoff to the non-goal.

The basic philosophy of this OS was to not force every user
to become a sysadmin or a system analyst.  PeeCees insist
that each and every user has to become a specialist in all
aspects of hard/software maintenance.  Now, you as a manger
can have your people spend most of their time working
on keeping each desktop running or you can have them do
the actual work by using software that aids the user and
doesn't create impassable roadblocks to getting work done.


Now I realize that you will have absolutely no idea what
I just wrote.  I'm speaking through you to the bit sitters.

