In article <3iath7Fhmt60U1@individual.net>, bill@cs.uofs.edu (Bill Gunshannon) writes:
> 
> So, you would agree to pay extra for the base OS in order to support
> the various users who use the parts you don't have any need for?  

Well, in principle yes. 
Most (if not all) other vendors do it this way,
and I haven't heard that you pay through your nose to get the base OS
of AIX, Solaris, HP-UX, IRIX, etc.
BTW we are not talking specialized exotic features here, 
but stuff like networking, GUI, possibly LVM/JFS features,
all of which you would need to run a modern OS.

> See,
> it goes both ways.  If you bundle everything then everything is more
> expensive.  If you let people only pay for what they need/want the
> product becomes more attractive.

Still doesn't answer my principle question.
IBM, Sun, pre-merger HP, etc don't have any problem to make
their products attractive without a bizarre component/licensing scheme.

> 
> At this stage of the game, I doubt that royalties to other people
> make up any sizeable amount of the cost of Solaris. I haven't seen
> the release of the source code yet so I don't know that there is
> anything in there that still draws royalties.  After all, they offer
> nothing that is not also offered by Linux and the various BSD's and
> they don't pay anyone royalties.  Is it so hard to assume that SUN
> long ago removed anything that was still proprietary beyond their
> own labs?

I don't know. But look at IBM, certainly a late-comer in the UNIX
business, they sure have licensed a lot of stuff from others
(judging from the copyright's scattered all over the OS).
And if Sun managed to remove royalty stuff to be cheaper or
less obligated, why can't DEC^WCompaq^WHP ?
 
> > 
> > Sure, it may give them excuses to stop this extra service
> > for a couple of hobbyist weirdo's.
> 
> Can I take it from this that you are not a hobbyist  but a business
> user and you think HP should let you use their IP to help you make
> money but they should not be afforded the same opportunity?

No you can't take that. If I were a serious business user I wouldn't
waste my time with a crippled hobbyist program (let alone endless discussions about it) 
but just pay for the real thing and move on. 
And I wouldn't call myself a hobbyist either, since
