Whether you see a reason to enforce it is immaterial.
What matters is whether HP sees a reason.

> Coming back to some of the quoted stuff:
> If I buy used equipment, then the first owner has paid the paychecks 
> of the producer, so there's nothing wrong with selling it to
> somebody else. And I see no real difference between a car,
> a hobbyist CD or even a PAK. Its all used equipment.

No, a PAK is not equipment.  A PAK is not even a license.
A license grant by HP is accompanied by a PAK, but those license
rights do not transfer if the PAK is moved.  Even if you legally
transfer a VMS license to someone else, they have to turn in the
PAK and get a new PAK as a result of the license transfer.

> Its mainly the IP lobby which forced law makers to produce laws,
> which, if applied to cars, would force customers to return
> their cars after use to the factory, instead of buying cheaper
> used ones, if they so wish.

The crucial phrase is "if applied to cars".  Nobody is attempting to
apply software licensing rules to car purchases.  Cars are hardware.


