Your mileage varies from mine.

>  DUNGEON = ZORK.

Or was supposed to, at least*.  On any system where I've encountered ZORK it was
called "ZORK,"  including the aforementioned system where I found ADVENT,
ADVENTURE, DUNGEON and ZORK all installed.  Typing DUNGEON resulted in a version
of ADVENTURE running.  And it wasn't just an installation decision, since it
referred to itself as "DUNGEON" in the output, but in operation it was clearly
ADVENTURE, including the usual magic words, house, grate, well house, Bedquilt
room and so forth.  ZORK likewise referred to itself as ZORK and was clearly NOT
a version of ADVENTURE.

> ADVENT is entirely separate.

Depends on the system, apparently.

> Clear enough now?

I'm aware of the history of these programs. I wasn't talking about technical
purity.  I was querying what the OP actually *meant* by the word.

    - Bill
______________
* "Zork" was originally MIT hacker jargon for an unfinished program. The
implementors named the completed game Dungeon, but by that time the name Zork
had already stuck.  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zork

This matches my memory of Zork development.




