Now - to add weight to what John seems to think ...

I'd gotten this "500au" from a pallet of old PCs and other stuff that
was going to recycle.
The guy had 3 extra CDROM drives and told me to take them "Just in
case, but it should run VMS or Tru 64Unix...".  1 CDROM was SCSI, the
other 2 are IDE.
AND - after finally looking at the front panel ... it says 500a.  My
apologies there.
I'm new to this hardware and when I swapped out the CDROM there it was
plain as day - 500a.   Also, looking at the inside - the SCSI cabling
is NOT original.   (I can only wonder what else might be trouble here.)

So - although I may die before VMS actually loads up (the drive is
S-L-O-W) it seems to be working.  I'll ask ONE LAST QUESTION though -
is there a FASTER CDROM drive I might get off eBay or other?  I'm the
type who does 4 or 5 installs for practice and fun before finally
leaving things alone, and don't HAVE this kind of time! ;)

