In article <oPadndj5F--wsC7fRVn-sw@giganews.com>, "Bill Leary" <Bill_Leary@msn.com> writes:
> I've always considered that H-11 to be a surprising machine in that Heath put a
> something beyond a micro in a box for the (almost) average consumer at a time
> when that sort of thing just wasn't being done, except by very hard core
> hobbyists.

I don't know how true this is, but I heard a story of how the H-11 came
about.

When KO started up DEC, they didn't have the bucks to buy all the test
equipment they needed. So they bought and built a bunch of Heathkits. Ken
had a soft spot for Heath ever since, and when the original LSI-11 was
obsolete, instead of scrapping all the boards, the sold them to Heathkit to
make H-11s.

The machine was WAY ahead of any home built computer of its day. 16 bits
instead of 8 bits, and FAST. My "benchmark" was a basic program to do my
model rocket flight simulation. The TRS-80 couldn't do anything mroe than 4
function calculator math, much less inverse hyperbolic cotangents. The best
I found was the Comodore PET, which took about 3.5 seconds to do the
calculations I needed to do hundreds (thousands) of times. The H11 was so
fast I had to put it in a loop to be able to time it with my stopwatch. IIRC
it was around a tenth of a second. And the compiled Fortran was a couple
orders of magnitude faster than the interpreted Basic I benchmarked with.

Could you imagine a company doing something like that today? Making last
years hardware available to hobbyists REALLY cheap?

