Rich Alderson <news@alderson.users.panix.com> writes:

>    ZORK/DUNGEON User Information
>    I. Getting a ZORK/DUNGEON
>        a. ITS:  Say ZORK^K to DDT rather than :ZORK to get a zork. 

This is a construction that I associate with the MIT hackers of
the '70s.  Where we would now say "run LISP" they would say "get
a LISP."  It treats each person's invocation of a program as a
separate thing.

I don't remember Guy Steele mentioning this little linguistic quirk
in THE HACKER'S DICTIONARY, although it might in the original Jargon
file.  It certainly hasn't survived into modern hacker talk.

Does anyone else remember this English construction in use anywhere
else besides MIT?

- David Librik
librik@panix.com


