> documentation produced by/on computer only had upper-case available, so
> lower-case only appeared in printed documents and books.

Yes, talking about non-computer printed documents here.

> I've always had
> the impression that McCracken's books of that era used to use caps/small
> caps.

You could be right.  I've just taken a look at his biography (
http://www.ccnyddm.com/FullBio.htm ) and I don't recall reading any of his books
on Fortran, though the name is familiar to me.  I may have read one of his works
on Pascal.

> It certainly wasn't any Pascallish influence; Wirth hadn't yet thought
> about anything other than Algol W.

At that time, sure not.  I was talking about the few times I've encountered the
bi-caps (ForTran or large/small caps) version myself.

Anyone got one of those McCracken works* handy they could flip open and take a
quick look at?  It's not hugely important, I'll admit, but it's interesting in
the historical sense.

    - Bill

PS: Well, that was interesting.  When I just hit SEND on this message, my
spell-checker wants me to change all instances of Fortran to FORTRAN throughout
this message.
