My main source of Ye Olde ForTranne Lore are scraps of vellum and
cracked clay tablets that are stored in a special vault at my
University Library. The walls of the vault are heavily scorched, but
there are still disturbing mural paintings visible, done in a flaky
reddish brown paint.

Anyway, at home I only have a Fortran 90/95 book:
Stephen J. Chapman, Introduction to Fortran 90/95, McGraw-Hill 1999

It emphasizes (gasp!) structured programming and disavows any knowledge
of the COMMON block, or implicit variable typing. 

In a footnote of SS 1.4, Chapman claims: "Versions of the language before
Fortran 90 were known as FORTRAN (written with all capital letters),
while Fortran 90 and later versions are known as Fortran (with only
the first letter capitalized)."

