> In article <d8d7n7$vqv$1@news01.intel.com>, Ken Fairfield
<my.full.name@intel.com> writes:
> > Schnootling wrote:
> >> Hi Folks,
> >>
> >> I noticed an Alpha 4100 available at (for my home, personal use) a
> >> reasonable price.
> >
> >      That would be a nice "home" system!  Up to 4 CPUs and 8GB of
> > memory (unless there are now even higher density memory options).
> > I/O is PCI.  Available CPUs were, IIRC, 330, 400 or 500 MHz EV56.
>
> The faster CPUs for it are 466, 533 and 600 Mhz.  The 600 has twice
> the cache (8MB) of the slower boards.  There are Windows NT only
> versions of the CPUs, but they can be made to run VMS with a simple
> work around.
>
> >      Do note that his "pedestal" is much larger than a PC in a
> > "pedestal".  It's something like desk height, about 24" wide (or
> > more likely 19"???) and about 30" deep.  This all from 5 year old
> > memory...
> >
> > However, before I look into it further, I'd like an
> >> (in)sanity check:
> >> 1) What are the power requirements for a 'loaded' Alpha 4100 (say 10
> >> disk drives and 1 tape drive with 2 CPUs) ? I've got U.S. standard
> >> wiring at my house, although I could certainly upgrade some of it.
> >
> >      I don't recall the actual _power_ requirements, but I do know
> > there will be 3 power supplies (2+1 redundant), each with a standard
> > US 110/120V 3-prong plug (at least ours were).
>
> With one or two CPUs, only one power supply is required (a second one
> provides redundancy).  More than two CPUs requires two power supplies
> with a 3rd one being redundant.
>
> Standard 15 amp wall outlets are fine, but I wouldn't use a circuit
> with much other stuff on it.  Off the top of my head, I believe the
> power supplies are rated at something like 450 watts each.
>
> Assuming you plan to use BA35x shelves for the disks and tape, at
> least two shelves would be needed.  Each shelf would need a 150 or
> 180 watt power supply depending on the disk models.
>
> >> 2) Also, would I be able to run hobbyist VMS on it ?
> >
> >      Of course :-)  Put a graphics card in it, and you have a sweet
> > Alpha*Station* 4100!  :-) :-)
>
> It probably has an 8-plane (256 colors) card in it unless it has been
> stripped.  There are very limited choices for 24-plane cards, but the
> 8-plane cards are not hard to find.
>
>
> George Cook
