> in running anything other than OpenVMS on it.)
> 
> It seems I might have to modify the Alpha (and thereby go unsupported
> by HP). Is this correct ?

If it is an Alpha 4100 (or 4000), it will run OpenVMS as is and be fully
supported by HP.  The only reason you would need to worry about the
Windows NT only CPUs, is if you wanted to upgrade to faster CPUs and
purchased cheaper NT only ones (very little market for them).

We picked up four 533Mhz NT only CPUs a while back for $100, and used
them to upgrade a 466Mhz two CPU 4100.  The latest VMS firmware and
VMS 7.3-2 run on it just fine.  The only problem is that it reports
its CPU model name as a "DIGITAL Server 7000 Model 7305 6533A 5/533 4MB".
There is actually a console command to set the model number, but it
has checks in it which we never figured out how to bypass.  We also
would of course be in trouble if we were required to have a commercial
VMS license for it.


George Cook
