The CO <the_xo@yahoo.com.au> wrote:
>Hans Bachner wrote:
>> Just for info - I have a 7305 (a "4100 in disguise") with only one power 
>> supply, 2 CPUs, and 512 MB memory. When I got it I measured the actual 
>> power consumption (with only 2 disks at that time) and it was something 
>> between 350 and 400 watts.

Estimate ~ 75 W per Alpha CPU, 10 W per modern SCSI disk, 20 W for 128+ MB
RAM, and 30-50 W for the backplane, network, and other I/O.
Which makes its power consumption comparable to an Intel 3+ GHz P4 with one
or two high-end video cards. No worries.

>The supply ratings are usually *very* generous.
>I was told this was so that no matter how many peripherals and plugins 
>you shove in it you could not possibly overload them.

Indeed. At one time I owned a VaxStation 3100 M38 (BA42-A enclosure) with
a 348 W rated PSU and a VaxStation 4000 M 60 with a 465 W rated PSU.
Both PSUs are designed to be able to provide enough Amperes to start up 4 or
more full height 5 1/4" hard disks (~12 V, 4 A start up). After start up
the consumption is a lot less, about 200 W apiece, much less with modern
3 1/2" hard disks.

scs


