 Rich Alderson <news@alderson.users.panix.com>  in
alt.folklore.computers wrote:

>First, let me point out that there is no such thing as "the PDP."  DEC sold
>four different families of computers whose names were PDP-n:  PDP-1/4/7/9/15
>(18-bit), PDP-5/8/12 (12-bit), PDP-11 (16-bit), and PDP-6/10 (36-bit).
>
>dunric@yahoo.com writes:
>
>> What systems did the Infocom game Dungeon/Zork run on? PDP-10? PDP-11?
>
>The *Infocom* Zork family of games ran on just about every microcomputer made
>in the 1980s; in the 1990s, they probably only ran on Windows and Macintosh.
>
>The *original* ZORK ran only on MIT's Incompatible Timesharing System and on
>TOPS-20 (though Stu Grossman wrote a TOPS-20 compatibility package for Tops-10
>at Stevens so that he could run ZORK there).
>
>DUNGEON was a Fortran port of ZORK done by (if I have this right) Bob Supnik.
>
>> I don't recall a version of "Advent" (Colossal Caves) either, for anything
>> other than the PDP-10 or 11. I don't recall any of these games being ported
>> to the PDP-8 system.
>
>They're really big.
>

The -10 version of ADVENT was ported to RT11 Fortran IV.  Magic mode
and some other bits were sacrificed to make it fit.  That was recoded
in RALF assembler for the PDP-8.   The load module (input to FRTS
loader) is 143 blocks (256*12), with a 200 block data file.  Ok, it
needed 32KW core, somewhat more than the typical -8 would have, until
8As with 16K-on-a-board  memory. 

I know that Zork was ported to RT11, but I never found a copy.

>> Any ideas as to why? Didn't the PDP-8 have at least 6 KB of core memory?
>
>A baseline PDP-8 had only 4K of 12-bit words; even maxed out it had only 32K,
>with (to a PDP-10 programmer) strange addressing tricks to get from one 4K
>field to another. 

Funny, I always considered the -10 a strange machine.  :)   If we look
at the user program space after the O/S has claimed its resident bit
then  OS/8 has over 32500 words available with 32K core. RT11 has 32K
words less 4K for the IO page and about 5K for SY: & RMON, so anything
that ports to RT has a fighting chance of running under OS/8. 

> However, one would think that people who could put Zork onto
>the 6502 (Apple ][ and Atari, for example) could have put it onto a PDP-8, if
>there had been any market for it.

Agreed, the -8 was pretty much a dead duck by then.  DEC were selling
a 128KW PDP8-A, but who would buy one in preference to a 22bit Qbus
machine?

Regards,

David P.



